.TH "PERFORMANCECONTAINERTIMING.INTERSECTIONRECT" "3JS" "October 26, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceContainerTiming.intersectionRect \-
PerformanceContainerTiming: intersectionRect property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]intersectionRect\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] interface returns the bounding
rectangle of the container\(cqs accumulated painted region, calculated
since the container root was registered.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]DOMRectReadOnly\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
The region defined by \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] may include gaps where
no painting has occurred, and omits content that was outside the
viewport when it was painted.
Paired with the \f[CR]size\f[R] property, which gives the accumulated
painted area, it provides a useful \f[I]indication\f[R] of how much of a
container has rendered and become visible so far.
It can also be used during debugging to confirm whether a container\(cqs
content is painting inside the viewport.
.PP
For more information, see When are entries generated?
in \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Observing that only visible paints are counted
This example demonstrates that content creates an entry only if some of
it is visible when painted: once new content is added below the visible
area of the container, no more entries are created.
It also demonstrates that the \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] and
\f[CR]size\f[R] values only ever increase.
.SS HTML
The container root (\f[CR]<section>\f[R], identified as
\f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R]) sits inside a fixed\-height \f[CR]<div>\f[R]
that hides any content that overflows it.
There is also a button to reset the example.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqreset\(dq>Reset</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqwrapper\(dq>
  <\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
    <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
  </\f[B]section\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS gives the wrapper a fixed height and hides any content that
overflows it.
.IP
.EX
wrapper {
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 150px;
  \f[B]overflow\f[R]: hidden;
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 1px solid black;
}
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
New log entries are added at the top of the log.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs each
entry\(cqs \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] and \f[CR]size\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] container = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);

\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      log(
        \(gaEntry: size: ${entry.size}, rectWidth: ${entry.intersectionRect.width}, rectHeight: ${entry.intersectionRect.height}\(ga,
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.PP
We then use \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to add a new paragraph to the
container every second, stopping after six paragraphs.
Each paragraph is logged when it is added, so you can see whether it is
followed by a new entry.
Note that we add content on a timer rather than when the user clicks a
button, because no entries are reported after the user interacts with
the page (see Reporting stops after scrolling or user input).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] maxParagraphs = 6;
\f[B]let\f[R] count = 0;

\f[B]const\f[R] timer = setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  count++;
  \f[B]const\f[R] paragraph = document.createElement(\(dqp\(dq);
  paragraph.textContent = \(gaNew paragraph ${count}\(ga;
  container.appendChild(paragraph);
  log(\(gaAdded paragraph ${count}\(ga);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (count >= maxParagraphs) {
    clearInterval(timer);
  }
}, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Last of all we add a click event handler to reset the example by
reloading the page.
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dq#reset\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  window.location.reload();
});
.EE
.SS Result
A new paragraph is added every second.
The first few paragraphs are painted inside the wrapper, so each is
followed by an entry with a larger \f[CR]size\f[R] and
\f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R].
Once paragraphs are added below the bottom edge of the wrapper, they are
clipped away, and are not followed by new entries.
Click the \f[B]Reset\f[R] button to restart the example.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming.size\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming.firstRenderTime\f[R]
